<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#mitma">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mitma</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as mitmaqkuna, mitimaes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mitma was a policy of forced resettlement used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>, in which extended families or whole ethnic groups were moved from their home territory, often hundreds of kilometres, to lands recently conquered, forming enclaves of settlers called mitmaqkuna. People of loyal provinces were sent to new or hostile territories and rebellious villages to consolidated regions, carrying loyalty to the state and Inca language and technology with them. Settlers had to keep their own dress and practices; one who tried to return home was tortured, and executed on a second attempt. Modern studies suggest that about a quarter to a third of the empire's population was resettled.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state spread Quechua as a common administrative language and moved whole communities as <b>mitmaqkuna</b>, settling loyal groups in unstable areas and relocating rebellious ones.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Incas moved whole communities from their homelands to other parts of the empire, a policy called <b>mitma</b>, and the settlers were known as mitmaqkuna.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li></ul></section>
